// =============================================================================
// 12-semantic-capacity-headroom.kql
// Estimated concurrent semantic requests against the computed in-flight ceiling.
// =============================================================================
//
// RUN THE DISCOVERY QUERIES FIRST
//   00-discover-dependency-types.kql and 01-discover-search-diagnostic-shape.kql.
//   Section 4 of file 01 decides whether the semantic-query filter below can be
//   applied at all. If Query_s is empty in your workspace, use the fallback in
//   COMPANION A, which counts all query operations and therefore overstates
//   semantic load.
//
// PURPOSE
//   Estimate how many Search requests were in flight concurrently in each time
//   bucket, and compare that estimate to the semantic ranker admission-control
//   ceiling for the service tier and search-unit count configured below.
//
// THE CEILING, AND WHY 6 IS THE NUMBER THAT MATTERS
//   Azure AI Search runs the semantic ranker behind a queue. Per search unit
//   (search units = replicas x partitions) the published limits are:
//
//     Tier     Max concurrent    Max queue    In flight before rejection
//     Basic          2               4                    6
//     S1             3               6                    9
//     S2             4               8                   12
//     S3             4               8                   12
//
//   When the concurrent limit is reached the service queues. When the queue is
//   full it rejects, and the caller sees 206 Partial Content carrying
//   @search.semanticPartialResponseReason.
//   On Basic at one search unit the ceiling is 6 in flight. The agent issues 7
//   concurrent semantic requests per user turn, one per knowledge source. A
//   single turn exceeds the ceiling before a second user arrives. That is the
//   strongest supported explanation for the 206s, and it explains why moving to
//   S1 helped so much: the ceiling moved from 6 to 9 per search unit.
//   It is the strongest supported explanation, not a confirmed cause. See the
//   caveats below.
//   Research: .copilot-tracking/research/2026-09-22/ai-search-scaling-206-403-rca-research.md
//             Lines 80-102 (D1, the limits table), Lines 103-116 (D2)
//
// TWO CAVEATS THAT MUST TRAVEL WITH THIS QUERY
//   1. Documentation conflict (open item U4). The service-limits page states
//      2 or 3 concurrent per SEARCH UNIT plus queue. The semantic ranking
//      how-to states 10 concurrent per REPLICA. Different magnitudes, different
//      units. They cannot both be literal. This query implements the limits
//      table. Raise the conflict with Microsoft support.
//   2. The concurrency estimate below applies Little's law
//      (concurrency = throughput x mean service time). It is an inference from
//      the logs, not a counter Microsoft publishes. Treat it as an indicator of
//      pressure, not as a measurement of the queue depth.
//
// TARGET TABLE
//   AzureDiagnostics, ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH".
//
// PREREQUISITES
//   * Search diagnostic setting sending OperationLogs to this workspace.
//   * Confirm the live replica and partition counts and set searchUnits below.
//   * Confirm properties.semanticSearch is "standard" and not "free" before
//     reading anything into this chart. The free plan allows 1,000 semantic
//     requests per month, which this workload consumes in days, and exhaustion
//     produces a billing error rather than a capacity signal.
//   Collect both with: scripts/Get-SearchServiceDiagnostics.ps1
//   Enable logging with: scripts/Enable-DiagnosticSettings.ps1
//
// HEALTHY VS UNHEALTHY
//   Healthy   : HeadroomPct stays comfortably positive and EstConcurrency sits
//               well below InFlightCapacity across the peak hour.
//   Unhealthy : EstConcurrency touches or crosses InFlightCapacity. Expect 206s
//               in the same buckets. Correlate against file 11 or file 21.
//   Action    : sustained saturation means either more search units, fewer
//               indexes per turn, or a provisioning request to Microsoft.
//               Microsoft documents the support ticket explicitly for workloads
//               at or above the semantic ranking ceiling.
//
// SIZING RULE OF THUMB
//   Size search units against ceil(7 x peak_concurrent_turns / 9) on S1, not
//   against average queries per second. Averages hide the per-turn burst.
// =============================================================================

// --- Tier parameters: edit these three to match the live service -------------
let lookback = 24h;
let bucket = 1m;
let searchUnits = 1;            // replicas x partitions at the time of the window
let maxConcurrentPerSu = 2;     // Basic = 2, S1 = 3, S2 = 4, S3 = 4
let maxQueuePerSu = 4;          // Basic = 4, S1 = 6, S2 = 8, S3 = 8
let inFlightCapacity = searchUnits * (maxConcurrentPerSu + maxQueuePerSu);
AzureDiagnostics
| where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
| where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
| where OperationName == "Query.Search"
// Semantic-only filter. Query_s is documented but UNVERIFIED in practice, so
// this predicate is the part most likely to need adjustment after file 01.
| where Query_s has "queryType=semantic" or Query_s has "semanticConfiguration"
| summarize
    Requests      = count(),
    AvgMs         = avg(DurationMs),
    P95Ms         = round(percentile(DurationMs, 95), 1),
    DistinctIdx   = dcount(IndexName_s)
    by bin(TimeGenerated, bucket)
// Little's law: concurrency = arrival rate x mean service time.
// The divisor 60.0 is the bucket width in seconds and must track the `bucket` value above.
| extend RequestsPerSec = Requests / 60.0
| extend EstConcurrency = round(RequestsPerSec * (AvgMs / 1000.0), 2)
| extend
    InFlightCapacity = inFlightCapacity,
    HeadroomPct      = round(100.0 * (inFlightCapacity - EstConcurrency) / inFlightCapacity, 1)
| project TimeGenerated, Requests, DistinctIdx, AvgMs = round(AvgMs, 1), P95Ms, EstConcurrency, InFlightCapacity, HeadroomPct
| render timechart with (ytitle = "Estimated concurrent semantic requests vs ceiling")

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION A: fallback when Query_s is empty
// Counts every query operation instead of semantic ones only. This OVERSTATES
// semantic load whenever the workload also issues non-semantic queries, so read
// the result as an upper bound.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// let lookback = 24h;
// let searchUnits = 1;
// let inFlightCapacity = searchUnits * (2 + 4);   // Basic
// AzureDiagnostics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
// | where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
// | where OperationName in ("Query.Search", "Query.Suggest", "Query.Lookup", "Query.Autocomplete")
// | summarize Requests = count(), AvgMs = avg(DurationMs) by bin(TimeGenerated, 1m)
// | extend EstConcurrency = round((Requests / 60.0) * (AvgMs / 1000.0), 2)
// | extend InFlightCapacity = inFlightCapacity
// | project TimeGenerated, Requests, EstConcurrency, InFlightCapacity
// | render timechart

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION B: the capacity-pressure triplet from platform metrics
// Throttle percentage, queries per second, and latency on one chart. This is
// the authoritative throttle signal; the resource log is the diagnosable one.
// Requires AllMetrics on the Search diagnostic setting.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// let lookback = 24h;
// AzureMetrics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
// | where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
// | where MetricName in ("ThrottledSearchQueriesPercentage", "SearchQueriesPerSecond", "SearchLatency")
// | summarize Value = avg(Average) by MetricName, bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)
// | evaluate pivot(MetricName, any(Value))
// | render timechart

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// COMPANION C: does ingestion collide with query time?
// The ingestion pipeline and the agent share the same search units. Indexing
// operations landing in the same buckets as degraded query latency is a second,
// independent capacity story worth ruling in or out.
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// let lookback = 24h;
// AzureDiagnostics
// | where TimeGenerated > ago(lookback)
// | where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
// | summarize
//     IndexingOps = countif(OperationName startswith "Indexing." or OperationName startswith "Indexers."),
//     QueryOps    = countif(OperationName in ("Query.Search", "Query.Suggest", "Query.Lookup", "Query.Autocomplete")),
//     QueryP95Ms  = round(percentile(DurationMs, 95), 1)
//     by bin(TimeGenerated, 5m)
// | render timechart with (ytitle = "Indexing vs query operations and query p95")
